<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" ?>
<!--
  FastDDS discovery profile — workstation <-> bernard-2.

  WHY THIS EXISTS
  Measured 2026-08-08 on the lab WiFi: multicast to 225.0.0.1 (the group
  `ros2 multicast send/receive` uses) crosses between the workstation and the
  robot fine, but multicast to 239.255.0.1 — the group FastDDS actually uses
  for SPDP discovery — is silently dropped by the access point. Everything
  else checks out: same subnet, no firewall, unicast fine both ways (ssh),
  both ends rmw_fastrtps_cpp, ROS_DOMAIN_ID=28 on both, no ROS_LOCALHOST_ONLY.

  So `ros2 multicast receive` succeeding is NOT evidence that DDS discovery
  will work. It tests a different group. Do not use it to rule this out.

  This file replaces multicast discovery with an explicit unicast peer list.

  THE maxInitialPeersRange TRAP
  An initial-peer locator with no port does not mean "reach every participant
  at this address". FastDDS expands it into one port per participant ID, and
  only covers IDs 0..maxInitialPeersRange, which DEFAULTS TO 4. The robot runs
  8 nodes, so with the default the last few participants are simply never
  discovered — and the failure is partial and silent: `ros2 topic list` returns
  most topics, missing exactly the ones belonging to late-starting nodes
  (/voltage, /cmd_vel, /joy, /Buzzer, /RGBLight, /edition, /JoyState).
  That looks like a flaky network and is not.

  Raised to 20 below. Anything above the node count on the busiest host works;
  the cost is a few more discovery datagrams per announcement.

  useBuiltinTransports=false additionally removes the shared-memory transport.
  That is the previous team's separate fix for the robot's container/host
  boundary (their /root/fastdds_unicast.xml), folded in here so one profile
  covers both problems. Note theirs is unreadable by user `jetson` and so has
  never actually been in effect for host-side nodes.

  USE
    export FASTRTPS_DEFAULT_PROFILES_FILE=/path/to/fastdds_unicast_peers.xml
  Must be set on BOTH ends. Setting it only on the workstation gets partial,
  unstable discovery: our announcements reach the robot, but the robot's own
  announcements still go only to the blocked multicast group.

  MAINTENANCE
  The addresses are hardcoded. If either machine's DHCP lease moves, discovery
  breaks with no error message. Prefer static leases; update here and nowhere
  else.
-->
<profiles xmlns="http://www.eprosima.com/XMLSchemas/fastRTPS_Profiles">

  <transport_descriptors>
    <transport_descriptor>
      <transport_id>udpv4_peers</transport_id>
      <type>UDPv4</type>
      <!-- See "THE maxInitialPeersRange TRAP" above. Default is 4. -->
      <maxInitialPeersRange>20</maxInitialPeersRange>
    </transport_descriptor>
  </transport_descriptors>

  <participant profile_name="unicast_peer_discovery" is_default_profile="true">
    <rtps>
      <userTransports>
        <transport_id>udpv4_peers</transport_id>
      </userTransports>
      <!-- Drops the default SHM + UDP pair in favour of the descriptor above.
           Also removes shared memory, which cannot cross a container boundary. -->
      <useBuiltinTransports>false</useBuiltinTransports>
      <builtin>
        <initialPeersList>
          <!-- bernard-2 (robot, WiFi) -->
          <locator>
            <udpv4>
              <address>10.0.0.107</address>
            </udpv4>
          </locator>
          <!-- dev workstation (wlan0) -->
          <locator>
            <udpv4>
              <address>10.0.0.117</address>
            </udpv4>
          </locator>
          <!-- Loopback: with multicast gone, this is how nodes on the same
               host find each other. Do not remove. -->
          <locator>
            <udpv4>
              <address>127.0.0.1</address>
            </udpv4>
          </locator>
        </initialPeersList>
      </builtin>
    </rtps>
  </participant>
</profiles>
